<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<ValidationRule xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <!--
        THIS NOTE LIVES IN A COMMENT, NOT IN <description>, AND IT MUST STAY THAT WAY.
        ValidationRule.Description is capped at 255 characters. The full rationale below is
        operationally important, so it was moved here rather than trimmed; a comment is
        repo-only and is never sent to the Metadata API, so it can be as long as it needs to
        be. Do not paste this back into <description>.

        Decision 4: a deal cannot be moved back to an earlier stage. ABSOLUTE for users,
        there is deliberately NO bypass permission and NO other admin/data-fix escape hatch
        beyond the two named carve-outs below. Do not reintroduce a bypass custom permission;
        the user explicitly rejected one at the design gate (D4 = NO bypass custom
        permission, "the block is absolute for users"). This rule is absolute against
        PERMISSIONS but NOT against PROCEDURE: because entering Dead/Pass (new rank 0) and
        leaving Dead/Pass (prior rank 0) are both always allowed (see the rank map below),
        any user with ordinary edit access on StageName can reach any earlier stage in two
        saves via Dead/Pass, for example PSA to Dead/Pass to Under Review, with no special
        permission required. That two-save round trip through Dead/Pass IS the actual
        recovery procedure for a mistakenly advanced deal, and it is far less drastic than
        deactivating this validation rule org-wide; deactivating the rule is NOT the remedy
        and should not be used. The Dead/Pass route defeats stage ORDERING only, not the
        substantive checks: every forward CONTENT gate this rule coexists with (NDA signed,
        Underwriting approved, LOI approved, contract signed) still fires on the way back up,
        so a deal walked back to Under Review this way still cannot reach PSA again without a
        signed NDA and an approved LOI. This is an unavoidable consequence of the
        user-required invariant that Dead/Pass stay reachable from every stage, not a defect
        to be fixed: a validation rule sees only PRIORVALUE versus the new value on a single
        save, so it cannot distinguish "legitimately moved to Dead/Pass to kill a deal" from
        "using Dead/Pass as a stepping stone back down the pipeline."

        RANK MAP (edit here AND in NDA_Signed_Before_Deal_Progression's stage list if a new
        stage is ever added): New=1, Under Review=2, Development Review=3 (Land only),
        Construction Review=3 (Commercial only, deliberately the SAME rank as Development
        Review; they are parallel record-type branches, not a sequence, and neither is
        reachable on the other record type), Underwriting=4, LOI=5, PSA=6, About to Close=7,
        Closed Won=8. Dead/Pass and any unmapped/future stage rank 0 = UNRANKED = the rule
        does not compare it. This is a deliberate fail-open default: entering Dead/Pass (new
        rank 0) makes the "new rank > 0" clause (clause 3) false so the rule never fires
        (Dead/Pass always reachable, per invariant); leaving/reviving Dead/Pass (prior rank 0)
        is allowed too, but not because of the "prior rank > 0" clause (clause 2). That clause
        is logically redundant for this case: since no rank is negative, clauses 3 and 4 (new
        rank > 0, and new rank < prior rank) already jointly imply prior rank > 0, including
        when leaving Dead/Pass, where the "is it backward?" clause (new rank < prior rank =
        new rank < 0) is already impossible on its own. Only clause 3 (new rank > 0) is
        load-bearing for the Dead/Pass invariant; clause 2 is kept in the formula anyway
        because it makes the intent explicit, it would matter if a negative rank were ever
        introduced, and it is the only clause removable without a behaviour change should the
        formula ever hit the 5,000-byte compiled-size ceiling. A future 10th stage with no
        rank mapping is therefore permissive, not catastrophic, it simply is not blocked,
        rather than freezing the whole pipeline the first time an admin adds a picklist
        value. TEXT(StageName) is used throughout rather than ISPICKVAL because Dead/Pass is
        stored as Dead%2FPass in picklist/BusinessProcess metadata but as Dead/Pass at
        runtime, TEXT() is a plain string compare and sidesteps the encoding question
        entirely.

        CARVE-OUT 1 (LOI-rejection approval path): PRIORVALUE(StageName) = 'LOI' becoming
        StageName = 'Underwriting' is exempted. LOI_Approval's finalRejectionActions performs
        exactly this transition on rejection. A currently-green test in this repo
        (LoiGateTest.rejectionReturnsDealToUnderwriting) demonstrates that approval-process
        field updates already bypass custom validation rules in this org, that transition
        would satisfy every clause of the deployed NDA gate (ISCHANGED true, stage IS
        Underwriting, NDA not signed) and yet the test asserts the stage lands cleanly on
        Underwriting. So this carve-out is belt-and-braces, not load-bearing under that
        finding, included anyway in case that finding does not hold in every org/config,
        since the cost of being wrong (a rejection getting blocked) is worse than the cost of
        an unnecessary carve-out. Re-verify in the target org before deploy: take a deal to
        LOI with an unsigned NDA, submit and reject the LOI approval, confirm the stage lands
        on Underwriting with no error.

        CARVE-OUT 1 STICKINESS CHECK (confirmed clean): CARVE-OUT 1 keys on
        PRIORVALUE(StageName) = 'LOI' AND StageName = 'Underwriting', i.e. a StageName
        TRANSITION, not a sticky field state. PRIORVALUE(StageName) is re-derived from the
        immediately prior stored value on every save, so it cannot go stale the way a
        never-cleared checkbox can; a later backward move from, say, PSA to Underwriting
        evaluates PRIORVALUE(StageName) = 'PSA', which does not match 'LOI', so CARVE-OUT 1
        does not apply and the rule blocks it normally. No ISCHANGED addition was needed
        here; the transition-keyed shape is self-limiting by construction.

        CARVE-OUT 2 (Opportunity_Initiate_Underwriting before-save Flow, AMENDED 2026-08-04
        to exclude Closed Won, see below): the clause
        NOT(AND(ISCHANGED(Initiate_Underwriting__c), Initiate_Underwriting__c,
        ISPICKVAL(StageName,'Underwriting'), NOT(ISPICKVAL(PRIORVALUE(StageName),'Closed
        Won')))) is exempted. That flow is a RecordBeforeSave, CreateAndUpdate flow with
        doesRequireRecordChangedToMeetCriteria = true and a filter of Initiate_Underwriting__c
        EqualTo true, i.e. it fires ONLY on the save where the checkbox TRANSITIONS to true,
        never on a later save where it is merely already true, whose one decision
        (Not_Yet_Underwriting: StageName not equal to 'Underwriting') assigns StageName =
        'Underwriting' whenever the record is not already there, reachable at the Flow level
        from ANY later stage (LOI, PSA, About to Close, Closed Won) unconditionally.
        Before-save Flow assignments run BEFORE validation rules evaluate, so without SOME
        carve-out this rule would see ISCHANGED(StageName) true and the new stage already
        rewritten to Underwriting by the flow, and would throw on every legitimate use of the
        checkbox, a regression in shipped, working behaviour. The flow itself is unchanged;
        this carve-out lives entirely in this rule.

        ISCHANGED(Initiate_Underwriting__c) IS LOAD-BEARING AND MUST NOT BE REMOVED: the flow
        never clears Initiate_Underwriting__c back to false after it fires (its single
        assignment touches only $Record.StageName), so the checkbox stays true on that record
        permanently. Without ISCHANGED(Initiate_Underwriting__c) the carve-out would grant a
        PERMANENT backward-move exemption into Underwriting to any deal that ever ticked the
        box once, indefinitely, which is exactly the sticky, unadministered bypass the user
        rejected at D4, just reintroduced through a different field. Adding
        ISCHANGED(Initiate_Underwriting__c) mirrors the Flow's own entry condition exactly,
        so the carve-out now fires on precisely the saves the Flow fires on and no others. Do
        not "simplify" this formula by dropping ISCHANGED(Initiate_Underwriting__c), that
        would silently reopen the permanent exemption. ISCHANGED(Initiate_Underwriting__c) is
        false on insert (nothing to change from), but the whole rule is already gated behind
        ISCHANGED(StageName), which is also false on insert, so the create path is unaffected
        either way.

        USER DECISION, CLOSED WON EXCLUSION (2026-08-04): the rewind stays allowed from LOI,
        PSA and About to Close, but is now BLOCKED from Closed Won. Rationale recorded
        verbatim: "this deal needs more underwriting" is a real scenario mid-pipeline, so
        breaking the checkbox entirely would be a regression in working behaviour. But a WON
        deal being silently reversed by any deal driver ticking a checkbox is a wider bypass
        than the admin custom permission the user explicitly rejected at the gate, it is
        available to more people and administered by nobody. Excluding Closed Won preserves
        the feature's legitimate use and closes the case that actually matters. The added
        clause NOT(ISPICKVAL(PRIORVALUE(StageName),'Closed Won')) inside the carve-out's own
        AND means the carve-out only applies when the PRIOR stage was not Closed Won; when it
        was, that inner AND is false, so NOT(inner AND) is true, which lets the outer rule's
        AND chain proceed to evaluate true (i.e. the rule blocks) exactly as if no carve-out
        existed for that case.

        KNOWN ROUGH EDGE, DOCUMENTED NOT FIXED: ticking the checkbox on a Closed Won deal
        will now throw the generic backward-block error ("A deal cannot be moved back to an
        earlier stage...") rather than a message naming the checkbox, because the before-save
        Flow rewrites StageName to 'Underwriting' before this rule evaluates and the rule
        then rejects the save through its normal backward-block path. This is accepted
        deliberately by the user; the message is slightly indirect for that specific path but
        the Flow is not modified and the message is not special-cased for it.
    -->
    <fullName>No_Backward_Stage_Movement</fullName>
    <active>true</active>
    <description>Blocks moving a deal to an earlier stage. Two carve-outs apply (LOI-rejection approval; Initiate Underwriting, blocked from Closed Won). No bypass permission exists. See the XML comment.</description>
    <errorConditionFormula>AND(
    ISCHANGED(StageName),

    /* prior rank */
    CASE(TEXT(PRIORVALUE(StageName)),
        &apos;New&apos;,1, &apos;Under Review&apos;,2, &apos;Development Review&apos;,3, &apos;Construction Review&apos;,3,
        &apos;Underwriting&apos;,4, &apos;LOI&apos;,5, &apos;PSA&apos;,6, &apos;About to Close&apos;,7, &apos;Closed Won&apos;,8, 0) &gt; 0,

    /* new rank */
    CASE(TEXT(StageName),
        &apos;New&apos;,1, &apos;Under Review&apos;,2, &apos;Development Review&apos;,3, &apos;Construction Review&apos;,3,
        &apos;Underwriting&apos;,4, &apos;LOI&apos;,5, &apos;PSA&apos;,6, &apos;About to Close&apos;,7, &apos;Closed Won&apos;,8, 0) &gt; 0,

    /* is it backward? */
    CASE(TEXT(StageName),
        &apos;New&apos;,1, &apos;Under Review&apos;,2, &apos;Development Review&apos;,3, &apos;Construction Review&apos;,3,
        &apos;Underwriting&apos;,4, &apos;LOI&apos;,5, &apos;PSA&apos;,6, &apos;About to Close&apos;,7, &apos;Closed Won&apos;,8, 0)
    &lt;
    CASE(TEXT(PRIORVALUE(StageName)),
        &apos;New&apos;,1, &apos;Under Review&apos;,2, &apos;Development Review&apos;,3, &apos;Construction Review&apos;,3,
        &apos;Underwriting&apos;,4, &apos;LOI&apos;,5, &apos;PSA&apos;,6, &apos;About to Close&apos;,7, &apos;Closed Won&apos;,8, 0),

    /* CARVE-OUT 1: the LOI-rejection transition (see comment above) */
    NOT( AND(
        ISPICKVAL(PRIORVALUE(StageName), &apos;LOI&apos;),
        ISPICKVAL(StageName, &apos;Underwriting&apos;)
    )),

    /* CARVE-OUT 2: the Opportunity_Initiate_Underwriting before-save Flow.
       NOT available out of Closed Won - see comment above. */
    NOT( AND(
        ISCHANGED(Initiate_Underwriting__c),
        Initiate_Underwriting__c,
        ISPICKVAL(StageName, &apos;Underwriting&apos;),
        NOT( ISPICKVAL(PRIORVALUE(StageName), &apos;Closed Won&apos;) )
    ))
)</errorConditionFormula>
    <errorDisplayField>StageName</errorDisplayField>
    <errorMessage>A deal cannot be moved back to an earlier stage. To stop work on this deal, move it to Dead/Pass instead.</errorMessage>
</ValidationRule>
